  "published": "2026-11-10T21:22:28.807Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Breaking changes:\n- The packages no longer have a `net8.0` build. An application on .NET 8 or .NET 9 gets a .NET Standard build, without the APIs that only the `net10.0` build has, such as `DateOnly` and `TimeOnly` support. To keep those APIs, move the application to .NET 10.\n- The `netstandard1.x` and `netstandard2.0` builds no longer list `JetBrains.Annotations` as a NuGet dependency. If your code uses its attributes and you got the package through DataStandardizer, add a reference to `JetBrains.Annotations` to your project.\n- Casting `Money` to `long`, `int`, `short` or `sbyte` now gives the amount in minor units instead of truncating it (12.34 USD gave 12, now 1234), once the calling code is recompiled; use `(long)(decimal)money` for the old result. Casts to `ulong`, `uint`, `ushort`, `byte`, `char` and `nuint` no longer compile (CS0619), and `(nint)money` gives minor units.\n- `Money.Create`, `Money.Parse`, `Money.TryParse` and `IsNationalCurrency` reject ISO 4217 funds codes (`BOV`, `CHE`, `CHW`, `CLF`, `COU`, `MXV`, `USN`, `UYI`), as their documentation says.\n- `Money.CompareTo` throws `ArgumentException` when the currencies differ, so sorting a collection that mixes currencies fails instead of ordering it by amount alone.\n- `Money` `==` and `!=` return what `Equals` returns and no longer throw `InvalidOperationException` when the currencies differ.\n- The `IConvertible` members of `Money`, which `Convert.ToDecimal(object)`, `Convert.ToInt32(object)` and their siblings call, convert the amount with the rounding the value carries, as `(decimal)money` does, instead of the amount before rounding.\n\nNew:\n- Explicit `(long)`, `(int)`, `(short)` and `(sbyte)` casts of `Money` give the amount in ISO 4217 minor units, e.g. 12.34 USD gives 1234. They throw `InvalidCastException` for a currency without minor units or an amount that isn't a whole number of them, and `OverflowException` when the result is out of range.\n\nFixed:\n- The `netstandard1.1` build depends on `System.Net.Http` 4.3.4 instead of 4.3.2, which has a known vulnerability.\n- Debuggers can step into the packages' source through Source Link.\n- Money.Parse and TryParse reject a value with more than one sign, or a sign inside parentheses.\n- Money.TryParse returns false, and Parse throws FormatException, for the generic currency sign ¤.\n- `Money.ToString(format, provider)` formats `G`, a null format and custom numeric formats with the number formatting of a `CultureInfo`, `NumberFormatInfo` or `MoneyInfo` provider, not the current thread's culture.\n- `Money.ToString(format, provider)` with a `CurrencyFormatInfo` provider supports the `C`, `H`, `I` and `N` formats and formats the rounded amount.\n- `CurrencyFormatInfo.CurrencyGroupSizes` returns a copy of the sizes and stores a copy of the array it is given, as `NumberFormatInfo.NumberGroupSizes` does, so a read-only instance, including `CurrencyFormatInfo.InvariantInfo`, can no longer be changed through the array.\n- Setting `CurrencyFormatInfo.CurrencyGroupSizes` to null throws `ArgumentNullException`, as `NumberFormatInfo.NumberGroupSizes` does.\n- Setting `CurrencyFormatInfo.CurrencyGroupSizes` to sizes that `NumberFormatInfo.NumberGroupSizes` rejects (an element less than 1 or greater than 9, other than a last element of 0) throws `ArgumentException` when the property is set, instead of when a value is formatted.\n- `Money.ToString()`, `Money.ToString(IFormatProvider)`, `IConvertible.ToString` and `Convert.ToString(Money)` format the amount with the rounding the value carries, as `ToString(string, IFormatProvider)`, string interpolation and the conversion to `decimal` do. A value created with a rounding precision no longer prints its unrounded amount.\n- On .NET Standard 1.1, a `Money` value formatted or parsed without a provider uses the culture of the current thread, as on the other targets, instead of the invariant culture.\n- `MoneyInfo.CurrentMoney` follows a change of the current culture to another `CultureInfo` with the same name.\n- `Money.Create` throws `ArgumentOutOfRangeException` for a rounding precision outside 0 to 28, instead of creating a value whose conversion to `decimal` fails later.\n- `IConvertible.ToType` of `Money` returns the value itself for `object` and `Money`, and the string that `ToString` gives for `string`.\n\nChanged:\n- Integrate Money with .Net's formatting and parsing infrastructure.\n- The netstandard1.x and netstandard2.0 builds now carry nullable annotations, so projects on C# 8 or later with nullable enabled get null warnings from them.\n- `MoneyInfo.GetMoneyInfo` may return the same read-only instance to callers that pass the same `CultureInfo`, and formatting with the current culture no longer takes a lock.",
